run.info.short-refused
# Family slice (plan 04-09, D-07): programs halt (loop, HLT, TSTR follower, idle units) and fault
# (loop, sticky guest fault), each under five slicing patterns (single steps, fixed 7-tick slices,
# prime-sized slices, seeded xorshift32 slices, one call) against a seam-stepped unit-by-unit
# reference: per call end, now, units, elapsed, overshoot, stop and the CPU digest; at sampled ends
# the composite, seven domain digests and storage; summed counters. edges: targets on a boundary,
# one tick after, below now, equal to now.
run.slice.halt.single
run.slice.halt.fixed-7
run.slice.halt.prime
run.slice.halt.seeded
run.slice.halt.one-call
run.slice.fault.single
run.slice.fault.fixed-7
run.slice.fault.prime
run.slice.fault.seeded
run.slice.fault.one-call
run.slice.edges
# Family buffers (plan 04-10, D-02, D-03, D-05, D-06, API-04): caller-owned video and audio buffers
# of ivs_machine_run. The capacity rule bytes_per_frame x (max(target - now, 0) / nominal frame + 1)
# is proven through the internal env seam with a synthetic layout (16 bytes per frame, 1000-tick
# frame); every refusal with the SP4 oracle plus a poisoned buffer arena left unchanged. Exact fit
# per channel equals a public-call twin; NULL with capacity, a buffer inside the instance, video
# overlapping audio and a buffer overlapping the report are BAD_ARG; the real layout accepts every
# capacity and writes nothing; the public call equals the seam with an equal env. run.prec.*:
# one case per neighbour of the two new ladder slots (D-05).
run.buffers.too-small-video
run.buffers.too-small-audio
run.buffers.exact-fit-video
run.buffers.exact-fit-audio
run.buffers.exact-fit-both
run.buffers.zero-layout
run.buffers.null-with-capacity
run.buffers.inside-instance
run.buffers.video-audio-overlap
run.buffers.report-overlap
run.buffers.real-layout-zero
run.buffers.entry-points-equal
run.prec.handle-over-buffer
run.prec.unsupported-over-buffer
run.prec.buffer-over-state
run.prec.range-over-capacity
# Family input (plan 04-11, D-12..D-16, A-04, A-08): the 32-byte event record, one event inside
